# Part D of the chapter 2 corpus (SEN0401, version 1.2.0): the computing and cryptographic terms the earlier parts rely on, the address, the
# exchange rate, the wallet - and then the whole Network branch (who takes part, and how a transaction travels).
# Executed with the namespace of sen0401_ch02_corpus_v1_2_0.py (W, Y, E, H, K, Qb, Qc, Qs, _E, _ADDR, _TXID, NODES, CHECKS come from there).

# ============================ additions found by the audit of terms used in parts A-C ============================
NODES += [
 ("BitsAndBytes", "Bit and byte", 3, "Cryptography", ("32 bytes are 256 bits", "A bit is one binary digit; eight bits make a byte. Sizes in Bitcoin are quoted in both, and 32 bytes are 256 bits.", ("len(bytes.fromhex('4a5e1e4baab89f3a32518a88c31bc87f618f76673e2cc77ab2127b7afdeda33b')) * 8", "256")), [
  (W, "A bit is the smallest unit of digital information: a single binary digit that is either 0 or 1. A byte is a group of eight bits, and it is the unit in which computers normally measure the length of data. The two units describe the same thing at different granularities, so one length can always be converted into the other by multiplying or dividing by eight. Every size that this chapter quotes, from the 32 bytes of a transaction identifier to the 256 bits of a private key, is a statement in one of these two units."),
  (Y, "The chapter cannot be read without them. The book says that the output of SHA256 is always 256 bits long whatever the size of the input, that an input refers to an earlier transaction by a 32-byte identifier, and that output indexes are 4-byte unsigned integers [AH]. These are the same kind of statement, and a reader who cannot move between the two units will think that a 256-bit hash and a 32-byte hash are different objects. Precise sizes also matter for a different reason: the room a transaction takes in a block is counted in bytes, and that is what its fee buys."),
  (E, "You meet both units in every tool. Bitcoin Core reports the size of Alice's transaction as 194, counting bytes; the book describes a key as a 256-bit number; a block header is 80 bytes, which is 640 bits; and the chapter's figures for the first synchronization of a node are given in gigabytes, which are multiples of the byte [AH]. Outside Bitcoin the same units measure files, memory and network speeds, where the bit is traditional for transmission rates and the byte for storage."),
  (H, "The conversion is a multiplication by eight. The identifier of the first block's only transaction is 32 bytes of data, and reading it with the standard library and multiplying its length by eight gives 256, so the two descriptions agree. A byte written in hexadecimal takes exactly two characters, because one hexadecimal digit stands for four bits, which is why a 32-byte value is printed as 64 characters, as the book notes when it says that it takes 64 hexadecimal characters to display 32 bytes [AH]."),
  (K, "Watch the capital letter and the prefix. A lowercase b usually means bit and a capital B means byte, so 1 MB and 1 Mb differ by a factor of eight. Prefixes are also ambiguous: a kilobyte may mean 1,000 or 1,024 bytes depending on the author, which is one reason the book quotes the block limit as a weight of 4,000,000 rather than in megabytes [AH]. And a byte is not a character: text becomes bytes only after an encoding is chosen, which is why the standard for payment requests has to say that each octet of the UTF-8 sequence is percent-encoded [B21]."),
 ]),
 ("HexadecimalNotation", "Hexadecimal notation", 3, "Cryptography", ("the digits 0-9 and a-f", "Hexadecimal writes a number in base 16 with the digits 0 to 9 and a to f; each digit stands for four bits, so one byte is exactly two hexadecimal digits.", ("(int('ff', 16), int('1d00ffff', 16), len(hex(255)[2:]))", "(255, 486604799, 2)")), [
  (W, "Hexadecimal notation writes numbers in base sixteen, using the ten digits 0 to 9 and then the letters a to f for the values ten to fifteen. Because sixteen is two to the fourth power, one hexadecimal digit corresponds exactly to four bits and two digits to one byte. This makes hexadecimal the usual way to print binary data for human eyes: it is far shorter than writing out bits and, unlike decimal, it lines up exactly with byte boundaries."),
  (Y, "Almost every value this chapter quotes is written in hexadecimal: the transaction identifier, the block hash, the private key, the address's underlying program, the compact form of the mining target. Without the notation these would have to be printed as enormous decimal numbers in which no part corresponds to a field. The notation is also what lets a reader check a length at a glance, since the book's remark that 64 hexadecimal characters display 32 bytes is just the statement that two digits make a byte [AH]."),
  (E, "The identifier of Alice's payment, 46620030...d1f96177, is 64 hexadecimal characters. The compact encoding of the first block's mining target is printed as 1d00ffff, and the same block's hash begins with a long run of zeros, which is only visible because the hash is written in hexadecimal. Outside Bitcoin, colours in web pages, memory addresses in debuggers and bytes in network traces are all written the same way."),
  (H, "Converting is a matter of weighted digits. The two-digit value ff stands for fifteen sixteens plus fifteen, that is 255, the largest value a byte can hold; the four-byte value 1d00ffff read as one number is 486,604,799. Python's standard library converts in both directions, with int(text, 16) reading a hexadecimal string and hex(number) writing one, and the small program above shows all three results together. Hexadecimal is case-insensitive as a notation, although Bitcoin's newer address format deliberately forbids mixing cases for a different reason, its checksum [B173]."),
  (K, "Two traps are worth naming. The first is that hexadecimal is only a notation: the identifier of a transaction is 32 bytes of data, not 64 characters of text, and a program that hashes the printed form instead of the bytes gets a completely different answer. The second is the order in which the bytes are written down, which Bitcoin does differently in different places; the concept of byte order explains that trap in detail."),
 ]),
 ("Entropy", "Entropy", 3, "Cryptography", ("how unpredictable a secret is", "Entropy measures how unpredictable a value is, counted in bits; a key chosen from 2**256 equally likely values has 256 bits of it, and a key chosen badly has far less.", ("(2 ** 256).bit_length() - 1", "256")), [
  (W, "Entropy, in the sense used in cryptography, is a measure of how unpredictable a value is to someone who does not know it. It is counted in bits: a value drawn uniformly at random from two equally likely possibilities has one bit of entropy, from four possibilities two bits, and from two to the power 256 possibilities 256 bits. Entropy is therefore not a property of the number itself but of the process that produced it; the same number may be unguessable if it was drawn at random and worthless if it was the year of someone's birth."),
  (Y, "Every guarantee in this chapter that rests on a secret rests on entropy. A private key is only safe because an attacker would have to search an unimaginably large space, and that space is large only if the key was really chosen at random. The book makes the point sharply: it advises the reader not to write their own code to create a random number, to use a cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator seeded with sufficient entropy, and it warns that any process that is less than completely random can significantly reduce the security of the private key and of the bitcoins it controls [AH]."),
  (E, "You meet entropy wherever a secret is generated: when a wallet creates a key, when a browser sets up an encrypted connection, when a system picks a password salt. The book compares the size of Bitcoin's private key space, two to the power 256, with the number of atoms in the visible universe, and says that picking a private key is in effect picking a number between 1 and two to the power 256 [AH]. The same chapter notes that the private key must be less than the order of the curve, a number only slightly smaller than that bound."),
  (H, "Counting entropy is counting possibilities. If a value is chosen uniformly from N equally likely possibilities it has the base-two logarithm of N bits of entropy, so a uniform 256-bit number has 256 bits, which the small calculation above confirms by taking the bit length of two to the power 256 and subtracting one. Weak choices collapse this number: a key derived from a six-letter lowercase word has at most the entropy of twenty-six to the sixth power, about twenty-eight bits, and a modern computer can try every one of those in seconds. The gap between twenty-eight and 256 bits is the whole difference between a secret and a formality."),
  (K, "Entropy cannot be judged by looking at a value. A key full of apparently random digits may have been produced by a predictable generator, and the result will look exactly as unguessable as a real one. Nor does more entropy help anywhere else: the chapter's hashes, identifiers and addresses are public values, and adding randomness to them changes nothing. Entropy matters only for the values that must stay secret, which in this chapter means the private key behind the signature."),
 ]),
 ("Nonce", "Nonce", 3, "Cryptography", ("a number used once to vary a result", "A nonce is a value, often a counter, that is changed to make a cryptographic function produce a different output; signing uses a secret one and mining a public one in the block header.", ("next(n for n in range(1000) if __import__('hashlib').sha256(('Hello, world! %d\\n' % n).encode()).hexdigest().startswith('0'))", "32")), [
  (W, "A nonce is a value that is varied in order to change the output of a cryptographic function without changing the data that is really being protected. The word is a contraction of number used once. The book introduces it in exactly this role: a variable used to vary the output of a cryptographic function, in that case to vary the output of the SHA256 commitment to a phrase [AH]. Two very different nonces appear in this chapter, and keeping them apart matters: the secret nonce used when a signature is made, and the public nonce in a block header that a miner changes while searching for a proof of work."),
  (Y, "A nonce is what turns a fixed, deterministic function into something that can be searched. A hash function always gives the same output for the same input, so a miner who wants a different output must change the input, and the nonce is the field reserved for that purpose. In signing, the nonce serves the opposite purpose: it makes two signatures over the same message different, which is necessary for the mathematics of the signature scheme to stay secure. The book describes the signing step as first generating a private nonce, and warns that the extra complexity of handling it has in some cases resulted in software accidentally leaking private keys [AH]."),
  (E, "The mining nonce is a field of the block header, four bytes wide, described by the book's glossary as a 32-bit field whose value is set so that the hash of the block will contain a run of leading zeros, while the rest of the fields may not be changed because they have a defined meaning [AH]. The chapter's own description of mining is the same idea in words: if the output does not match the template, the miner makes a small change to the candidate block and tries again [AH]. The signing nonce is not stored anywhere: it is used once and discarded."),
  (H, "The book's own miniature example can be run. Setting the goal that the hexadecimal hash of the phrase Hello, world! followed by a number should begin with a zero, and trying the numbers in order, the first that succeeds is 32, giving the digest 09cb91f8...ccbec314, exactly the value and the nonce that the book prints [AH]. One hash verifies the answer, while thirty-two were needed to find it, and that asymmetry is the whole of proof of work. The mining nonce has only about four billion possible values, so real miners exhaust it and then change another field, which the book calls the extra nonce solution [AH]."),
  (K, "The two nonces must never be confused, because their rules are opposite. The mining nonce is public and may be anything; the signing nonce must be secret and must never be reused, since reusing one with the same key allows the private key to be recovered. A nonce is also not a password or a key: in mining it is simply the part of the input that the searcher is allowed to change, and in signing it is a one-time random value that is thrown away as soon as the signature exists."),
 ]),
 ("Encryption", "Encryption", 3, "Cryptography", ("making data unreadable without a key", "Encryption transforms data so that only a holder of the right key can read it again; Bitcoin's public-key cryptography is used for signatures, not for encrypting transactions.", ("(lambda k, m: (bytes(c ^ k for c in m).hex(), bytes(c ^ k for c in bytes(c ^ k for c in m)).decode()))(0x5a, b'pay Bob')", "('2a3b237a183538', 'pay Bob')")), [
  (W, "Encryption is the transformation of data into a form that cannot be read without a key, together with the matching transformation that turns it back. Its purpose is secrecy. It is one of several things that cryptography can provide, and this chapter mentions it mainly in order to say what Bitcoin does not do with it. The term is needed because two of the tools explained here, the hash function and the digital signature, are regularly mistaken for encryption, and the mistake leads to wrong expectations about what the system protects."),
  (Y, "The distinction decides what is private on a blockchain. The book states plainly that public-key cryptography in Bitcoin is not used to encrypt, that is, to make secret, the transactions, and that its useful property is instead the ability to generate digital signatures [AH]. Every transaction in this chapter, including Alice's, is therefore readable by anyone: the amounts, the conditions on the outputs and the signature itself are public. What a reader cannot do is forge a signature, and that is a guarantee about authorship, not about secrecy."),
  (E, "Encryption is met all around Bitcoin even though it is not inside a transaction. A browser connecting to Bob's shop uses it, a wallet file on disk may be encrypted with a passphrase, and the book's network chapter describes encrypted and authenticated connections between nodes, so that an observer of the wire cannot read what two peers exchange [AH]. The course notes for this chapter make the same separation when they describe the key as what unlocks a previous output rather than as something that hides data [AL]."),
  (H, "The simplest possible illustration is a reversible operation with a key. Combining each byte of the words pay Bob with the key value 0x5a by the exclusive-or operation gives the unreadable bytes 2a3b237a183538, and applying the very same operation again with the same key returns the original text, as the program above shows. Real encryption uses far stronger constructions, but the shape is this: one operation forward with a key, one back with a key. A hash function has no such backward step and no key, and a signature does not hide the message it is attached to."),
  (K, "Do not expect encryption to give privacy on the blockchain, and do not expect signatures to give secrecy. The book's chapter on keys lists the private key's requirement as remaining secret at all times, which is a matter of storage and not of encrypting transactions [AH]. Note also that encrypting a payment would defeat the system's purpose: nodes must read a transaction in order to verify it, which is why the data is public and the privacy of a user rests on other things, such as the fact that an address names no person."),
 ]),
 ("Serialization", "Serialization", 3, "TransactionPart", ("the 194 bytes of Alice's payment", "Serialization is the writing of a structure as a flat sequence of bytes in a fixed order, which is the form in which a transaction is sent, hashed and stored.", ("int.from_bytes(bytes.fromhex('204e000000000000'), 'little')", "20000")), [
  (W, "Serialization is the writing of a structured object as a flat sequence of bytes, in an order and a format on which sender and receiver agree, so that the receiver can rebuild the same structure. A transaction in a wallet is a structure of inputs, outputs and amounts; a transaction on the network is a string of bytes. The book names this form directly, calling serialized Bitcoin transactions the data format that software uses for sending transactions [AH]."),
  (Y, "Everything exact in this chapter depends on the serialized form. The identifier of a transaction is a hash of its bytes, so the bytes must be defined to the last detail; the size that a fee pays for is the length of those bytes; and the signature commits to them. If two programs serialized the same transaction differently they would compute different identifiers and the network would not work. This is why the description of the format belongs to the consensus-critical part of Bitcoin and not to a matter of taste."),
  (E, "The reader meets the serialized form as a long hexadecimal string. The book prints Alice's real transaction in that form in its chapters on Bitcoin Core and on transactions, and the same string appears in both; it is 194 bytes long, and every number this course quotes about that transaction was read out of it [AH]. Wallets exchange this form when a transaction is signed on one device and sent from another, and block explorers offer it for download."),
  (H, "The format is a sequence of fixed and variable fields. Amounts are eight-byte integers written with the least significant byte first, so the change output's 20,000 satoshis appear in the bytes as 204e000000000000, and reading those eight bytes back as a little-endian integer gives 20000, as the calculation above shows. Counts, such as the number of inputs, are written in a compact form that uses one byte for small values and more for larger ones. Reading the whole string field by field recovers the one input, the two outputs and the witness, which is how the checks of this chapter obtain the amounts without trusting any figure in the text."),
  (K, "A serialized transaction is not readable by eye, and it is not encrypted either: it is simply a precise encoding, and anyone with the format can decode it. Two cautions follow. First, the identifier is computed from the serialization without the witness data, so the same payment has two byte-lengths and two hashes, which the concepts of the identifier and of the size explain. Second, the hexadecimal string is a printing of the bytes, not the bytes themselves, so a program must decode the text before hashing it."),
 ]),
 ("ByteOrder", "Byte order", 3, "TransactionPart", ("the identifier read both ways", "Byte order is the direction in which the bytes of a value are written; Bitcoin displays hashes in one direction and stores them in the other, so the same value looks like two.", ("bytes.fromhex('4ac541802679866935a19d4f40728bb89204d0cac90d85f3a51a19278fe33aeb')[::-1].hex()[:16]", "'eb3ae38f27191aa5'")), [
  (W, "Byte order, also called endianness, is the direction in which the individual bytes of a multi-byte value are written down. Writing the most significant byte first is called big-endian and is what people do when they write ordinary numbers; writing the least significant byte first is called little-endian and is what most processors do internally. Both orders describe the same value, and a reader must know which one is meant, because the two printings look like completely different numbers."),
  (Y, "Bitcoin uses both orders, and the mismatch is a standing source of confusion that the book itself warns about: hash digests are displayed to users in one byte order but are used internally in a different byte order [AH]. A student who does not know this will conclude that the identifier stored inside Alice's input is a different transaction from the one her wallet shows, and a programmer who does not know it will search a block explorer for a hash that exists and find nothing."),
  (E, "The clearest example is in this chapter's own data. The identifier of the transaction in which Joe paid Alice is printed by the book and accepted by Bitcoin Core as 4ac54180...8fe33aeb, while the bytes stored inside Alice's input, read in the order in which they appear, spell eb3ae38f...8041c54a [AH]. The amounts in a serialized transaction are stored little-endian, and so are the four-byte fields of a block header, while a hash is displayed big-endian; both forms occur within the same 194 bytes."),
  (H, "Reversing a sequence of bytes converts between the two readings. Taking the displayed identifier, decoding it from hexadecimal into 32 bytes, reversing them and printing them again gives the stored form, whose first eight bytes are eb3ae38f27191aa5, as the calculation above shows; doing it a second time returns the original. The reversal is on whole bytes, not on hexadecimal characters: reversing the characters of the string would exchange the two halves of every byte and give a different value altogether."),
  (K, "Three practical rules follow. Reverse bytes, never characters. Reverse only values that the convention requires, which in this chapter means transaction and block hashes, not the witness or the output scripts. And when a tool reports something that does not match, try the other order before concluding that the data is wrong, because the book's note about the two byte orders applies to almost every hash a reader will paste from one program into another [AH]."),
 ]),
 ("Outpoint", "Outpoint", 3, "TransactionPart", ("txid 4ac54180... index 1", "An outpoint is the pair of a transaction identifier and an output index that names exactly one output; an input consists of an outpoint and the data that satisfies it.", ("(lambda txid, vout: '%s:%d' % (txid[:8] + '...', vout))('4ac541802679866935a19d4f40728bb89204d0cac90d85f3a51a19278fe33aeb', 1)", "'4ac54180...:1'")), [
  (W, "An outpoint is the exact name of one transaction output: the identifier of the transaction that created it together with the position of that output within the transaction. The book's chapter on transactions uses the word for the field inside an input and states its shape, an outpoint containing a 32-byte transaction identifier followed by an output index that is a four-byte unsigned integer starting from zero [AH]. The chapter's figures write an outpoint in the short form Tx1:0, a transaction label and a position separated by a colon."),
  (Y, "The outpoint is the link that makes the whole structure of Bitcoin work. Because an input names one and only one earlier output, a node can look it up, learn the amount and the spending condition, and check whether it is still unspent; and because the identifier is a hash, the reference cannot be redirected to other content. The set of unspent outputs that a full node keeps is in effect a table indexed by outpoints, so the double-spend check is a lookup of one outpoint."),
  (E, "The reader meets outpoints in block explorers, where every input is shown with the transaction it spends and the position within it, and in the chapter's two figures of transaction chains, where arrows join an output to the input that consumes it [AH]. In Alice's real transaction the outpoint names the transaction 4ac54180...8fe33aeb and the output at index 1, and Bob's later transaction, the chapter says, spends Tx2:1, the output of 75,000 satoshis."),
  (H, "An outpoint is formed by putting the two parts together, as the small example does by joining an abbreviated identifier and the index with a colon. In the serialized bytes the two parts sit next to each other: 32 bytes of hash, stored in the reversed order that the concept of byte order describes, then four bytes holding the index as a little-endian integer. Nothing else is needed, because the amount and the conditions are not repeated in the input; the book stresses that transactions do not explicitly include the value of their inputs and that software must find the previous output [AH]."),
  (K, "Two warnings. The index counts from zero, so the first output of a transaction is output 0 and the familiar off-by-one error here means spending the wrong output or none at all. And an outpoint is not an address and not an owner: it identifies a position in the ledger, and who may spend it is decided by the script in the output it names, which is why a chain of outpoints can be followed without learning anybody's identity."),
 ]),
 ("Address", "Bitcoin address", 3, "PaymentRequest", ("bc1qk2g6u8p4qm2s2lh3gts5cpt2mrv5skcuu7u3e4", "A Bitcoin address is a short string, carrying a checksum, that encodes the condition under which a payment may later be spent; it is what a payer is given and what a wallet turns into an output script.", ("(lambda a: (a[:3], len(a), a.islower()))('bc1qk2g6u8p4qm2s2lh3gts5cpt2mrv5skcuu7u3e4')", "('bc1', 42, True)")), [
  (W, "A Bitcoin address is a short string of letters and digits that stands for the condition under which a payment can later be spent. The book's glossary describes an address as a string of letters and numbers that is really an encoded form of a hash related to a public key, and says that you ask others to send you bitcoin to one of your addresses much as you ask them to send email to your email address [AH]. The address in this chapter, bc1qk2g6u8...uu7u3e4, is written in the newer format defined by BIP 173, which the book calls bech32 [B173]."),
  (Y, "An address exists for the payer's convenience and safety. A payer does not need to know anything about scripts, keys or hashes; the wallet turns the address into the output script that encumbers the payment, and the checksum inside the address catches a mistyped character before any money moves. The book makes the consequence explicit: the checksum prevents a mistyped address from being accepted by the wallet software as a valid destination, an error that would otherwise result in loss of funds [AH]."),
  (E, "The address is the first thing a reader meets in the chapter, as the path component of the invoice's payment link, and the chapter lists it among the components of that link together with the amount, the label and the description [AH]. Block explorers accept an address as a search term, the chapter says, along with a transaction hash, a block number or a block hash. Addresses also appear on receipts, on printed codes in shops and in the course notes for this chapter, where the older style beginning with a 1 is used [AL]."),
  (H, "The newer format is readable in three parts. A human-readable prefix, here bc for the main Bitcoin network, is separated by the digit 1 from the data, which encodes a version number and a program of twenty bytes, and ends with six characters of checksum [B173]. The program in Bob's address is b291ae1c...9485b1c, and the checksum calculation over the whole string returns the value that BIP 173 requires for a valid address. The small example above reads off the three obvious features: the prefix bc1, the length of 42 characters, and the fact that the address is written entirely in lower case, which the format recommends."),
  (K, "An address is not an account and not an identity. It holds no balance of its own, since a balance is the sum of the unspent outputs that a wallet can spend, and it names no person, which is why following a chain of transactions does not by itself reveal who the parties were. Addresses are also meant to be used once: the book explains that a change address is usually a new address of the sender's wallet for privacy reasons [AH]. Finally, the address in the book's invoice is an illustration: a check of the real transaction shows that its payment output encodes a different twenty-byte program, so the printed address did not receive the printed payment."),
 ]),
 ("ExchangeRate", "Exchange rate", 3, "PaymentRequest", ("a dollar price shown in bitcoin", "An exchange rate is the price of one currency in another; Bob's shop lists prices in dollars and converts them to bitcoin at checkout, so the bitcoin amount of an invoice is only valid for a moment.", ("round(25.0 / 1584.0, 8)", "0.01578283")), [
  (W, "An exchange rate is the price of one unit of a currency expressed in another currency. In this chapter it is the number that turns a price in United States dollars into an amount of bitcoin. The chapter says that the prices at Bob's store are listed in the local currency, US dollars, but that at checkout customers have the option of paying in either dollars or bitcoin, and that the checkout cart displays the price in dollars and also in bitcoin at Bitcoin's prevailing exchange rate [AH]."),
  (Y, "The rate is where Bitcoin meets the ordinary economy, and it explains a figure that otherwise looks arbitrary. The amount in the invoice, 0.01577764 bitcoin, is not a round number because it is the dollar price of the podcast episode converted at the rate of that moment. It also explains why a merchant who quotes in a local currency carries no currency risk on the listed price while a merchant who quotes in bitcoin does. The chapter earlier notes that people accept a money only if they expect to be able to spend it later, which is a statement about value over time [AH]."),
  (E, "You meet the rate on every checkout page that offers bitcoin, in every wallet that shows a fiat equivalent beside an amount, and in the figures of this chapter. The book's first chapter describes the same step from the other side, when Alice buys bitcoins from Joe for cash at an agreed price [AH]. Exchange rates are also what make the chapter's dollar comparisons meaningful, such as the coffee-shop analogy in which card payments below twenty-five dollars are accepted without a signature [AH]."),
  (H, "The arithmetic is a division. If a podcast episode costs twenty-five dollars and one bitcoin is worth 1,584 dollars at that moment, the price in bitcoin is 25 divided by 1584, which rounds to 0.01578283 bitcoin, a figure of the same shape and size as the invoice's amount. The dollar price and the rate here are an illustration chosen to show the calculation, not figures from the book; what the book gives is the converted amount. Converting the other way, an amount in bitcoin multiplied by the rate gives a price in dollars, and the same amount in satoshis is the bitcoin amount multiplied by one hundred million."),
  (K, "A rate is a market price at an instant, and it is the one number in a payment request that goes stale. An invoice that was correct when it was displayed may be worth more or less a few minutes later, which is why merchants normally let a quoted bitcoin amount expire. The rate is also not part of Bitcoin: no rule of the protocol knows about dollars, and the figures printed in the book are a snapshot of 2023. Treat a rate as context for reading the chapter's amounts, never as a constant."),
 ]),
 ("Wallet", "Wallet", 3, "Construction", ("the application that holds Alice's keys", "A wallet is the software that keeps a user's keys, tracks the outputs they can spend, builds and signs transactions, and shows balances; it holds keys, not coins.", ("(lambda utxos, pay, fee: sum(utxos) - pay - fee)([100000], 75000, 5000)", "20000")), [
  (W, "A wallet is the software through which a person uses Bitcoin. The book's glossary describes it as software that holds all your Bitcoin addresses and secret keys and that you use to send, receive and store your bitcoin [AH]. In practice it does four things: it keeps the keys, it keeps track of which outputs those keys can spend, it builds and signs transactions, and it presents the result to the user as a balance and a history. In this chapter there are three of them, Alice's, Bob's and, in the figures of the chain, Joe's and Gopesh's."),
  (Y, "The wallet is where all the complexity of the chapter is hidden from the user. The book says that Alice's wallet application contains all the logic for selecting inputs and generating outputs to build a transaction to her specification, and that she only needs to choose a destination, an amount and a transaction fee while the rest happens without her seeing the details [AH]. Everything a reader has met so far, coin selection, change, fees, scripts and signatures, is a decision that this program makes, which is why trusting a wallet is as consequential as trusting a bank used to be."),
  (E, "The chapter meets wallets at every step: Alice's wallet reads the payment request and prefills the payment, builds the transaction and sends it; Bob's wallet recognises the incoming payment because it contains an output redeemable by Bob's keys, and can independently verify that the transaction is well formed [AH]. The book distinguishes wallets that run on a full node, which hold a copy of every confirmed transaction's unspent outputs, from the many user wallets that run as lightweight clients and track only the user's own [AH]; and it adds that a wallet can build a transaction while completely offline."),
  (H, "The arithmetic a wallet performs for a payment is the subtraction of this chapter. Given the outputs it controls, the amount to pay and the fee to leave, it works out the change: with one output of 100,000 satoshis, a payment of 75,000 and a fee of 5,000, the change is 20,000, which is what Alice's real transaction contains. Around that subtraction sit the steps described elsewhere in this branch: choosing which outputs to spend, writing an output script for the receiver, writing one for the change, and signing each input with the private key that the output being spent demands."),
  (K, "A wallet does not contain bitcoins. It contains keys and a view of the ledger, which is why a backup of the keys restores the funds and a copy of the balance does not. Two further cautions belong here. A wallet that is offline cannot know whether an output it believes it controls has already been spent, so a transaction built offline is only a candidate until the network accepts it. And the user cannot see what the wallet decided, so a defect in the software can overpay a fee or leak a change address without any warning; the book's chapter on fees asks wallets to make an excessive fee rate hard to pay by accident [AH]."),
 ]),
 ("Witness", "Witness", 3, "TransactionPart", ("69 of Alice's 194 bytes", "The witness is the data that proves an input may be spent, kept in its own structure outside the part that the transaction identifier is computed from; this separation is called segregated witness.", ("(lambda total, stripped: (total - stripped, stripped * 3 + total))(194, 125)", "(69, 569)")), [
  (W, "The witness of an input is the data that proves the input may be spent. The book gives the word its general sense, saying that an abstract name for the data held by an input script is a witness, and then names the arrangement that this chapter's transaction uses: the idea of separating the rest of the data in a transaction from its witness for the purpose of generating a transaction identifier is called segregated witness, or segwit [AH]. The field that holds the separated data is called the witness structure [AH]."),
  (Y, "The separation matters for two reasons that this chapter has already met. The first is the identifier: because the witness is left out of the data that is hashed, the identifier of a transaction no longer changes when the proof changes, which removes a family of problems the book lists as circular dependence and third-party and second-party malleability [AH]. The second is size: the witness bytes are counted once in the weight while the rest are counted four times, which is why spending an output is cheaper than it would otherwise be and why the three size figures of a transaction differ."),
  (E, "The reader meets the witness in the bytes of Alice's transaction, which is written in the newer format. Two extra fields at the front mark that format, which the book calls the extended serialization: a marker that must be zero and a flag that in the current protocol is one, and the book notes that a transaction with no witness must instead use the older, legacy serialization, which has neither those two fields nor the witness structure [AH]. The witness in Alice's transaction holds one item of 65 bytes, the signature and its hash-type byte, as the digital-signature concept explains."),
  (H, "The figures can be read out of the transaction. Its full serialization is 194 bytes and the part without the witness is 125, so 69 bytes belong to the witness and the two marker bytes; the weight is the stripped part counted four times plus the witness part once, which the expression computes as 125 times three plus 194, that is 569, the figure Bitcoin Core reports, and dividing by four and rounding up gives the 143 virtual bytes [AH]. The transaction identifier is the double hash of the 125-byte stripped form, which is what Bitcoin Core's source does when it hashes the serialization without witness data [BC]."),
  (K, "Do not read the separation as hiding: the witness is public, it is sent to every node and it is committed to by the block, so nothing about it is secret. Note also that the word has two uses that the book keeps apart, the witness as the abstract name for authorizing data and the witness structure as the field that carries it, and that the course's later chapters on authorization open both. Finally, a transaction with no witness at all is serialized in the older format, so a program that assumes the two marker bytes are always present will misread such a transaction [AH]."),
 ]),
]

